\C{build} Building RxDOS 7.20N from the source code

\H{} Required tools

\n NASM 2.04 .. 2.10, other versions might work

\n One of these linkers:

\lcont{
\n WarpLink 2.70

\n ALink, possibly

\n Other 16-bit OMF linkers, with trivial adjustments

\n NASM's internal linker (output format \c{bin}), requiring some adjustments to
 the source code and scripts
}

\n exe2bin (tested with OpenWatcom 1.6 .. 1.9 program)

Note that usage of NASM's internal linker would make exe2bin unnecessary too. Some
other linkers will output a flat binary instead of an .EXE file too.

\H{} Warnings to expect

Recent attempts to assemble the provided source code (with NASM 2.10rc1\-20101106)
caused several warnings in RxCMD.ASM, mostly by re-using variable names
without manually undefining these pre-processor variables. The reason that this
was not yet fixed might have been that RxCMD wasn't finished yet. Some (but probably
not all) of these cases warn with "trailing garbage after expression ignored"
because the %[i]assign directives get passed something like "\cw{_-2\_-2}" (as %1 is
already expanded). A simple solution to these problems (in the macros) requires
the %# feature that is not yet implemented in NASM.

The "word data exceeds bounds" warnings that are displayed for RxCMD.ASM too
can probably be ignored.

The erroneous relocation in the RxDOS.EXE file as reported by exe2bin is,
though non-sensical, indeed in the source.

\H{} Script

The mak.cmd script should be sufficient to create a makefile or simple script
for your particular platform. It was created for a Windows NT environment.

To achieve mostly binary-compatible files to RxDOS 7.1.5 the automatic optimization of recent
NASM versions needs to be turned off using the command line switch "\cw{-O0}".

\S{simplebin} Building RxBIO, RxVDISK, RxBOOT, RxMBR

The associated .SYS file has to be build from the .ASM source code file using NASM's
output format \c{bin}. As for all NASM command lines, \c{-i..\\MACRO\\} or \c{-i../MACRO/}
has to be specified
to pass the macro include path to NASM. Minimal example:

\c nasm -i..\MACRO\ -o..\bin\RxBIO.SYS RxBIO.ASM
\c nasm -i..\MACRO\ -o..\bin\RxVDISK.SYS RxVDISK.ASM
\c  .
\c  .
\c  .

\S{} Building RxD_BOOT

First, the intermediate file RxD_BOOT.SYS has to build like the other files described in \k{simplebin}:

\c nasm -i..\MACRO\ -o..\bin\RxD_BOOT.SYS RxD_BOOT.ASM

Then, the file RxBOOT.SYS has to be appended to RxD_BOOT.SYS to form RxD_BOOT.COM. A
\c{copy} command might require a \c{/b} switch to specify that it should open the
files in binary mode. The \c{/y} is used here to specify overwriting
the destination if it exists.

\c copy /y /b RXD_BOOT.SYS + RXBOOT.SYS RXD_BOOT.COM

\S{builddosobj} Building RxDOS

Each of the RxDOS modules RxDOS, RxDOSCCB, RxDOSDEV, RxDOSEXE, RxDOSFAT, RxDOSFCB,
RxDOSFIL, RxDOSLFN, RxDOSF32, RxDOSIFS, RxDOSMEM, RxDOSSFT, RxDOSSTR, RxDOSCTB,
RxDOSERR and RxDOSINI need to be assembled into intermediate objects file first. For example:

\c nasm -i..\MACRO\ -fobj -o..\obj\RxDOS.OBJ RxDOS.ASM
\c nasm -i..\MACRO\ -fobj -o..\obj\RxDOSCCB.OBJ RxDOSCCB.ASM
\c  .
\c  .
\c  .

Then, all the object files have to be linked together with a linker. This is the command line
that works with WarpLink:

\c warplink @warplink.lin

The file warplink.lin contains the list of files and command line options. The order
in which these files are linked is important. RxDOS.OBJ must be the first, while RxDOSINI.OBJ
must be the last. The order of the other files probably doesn't matter.

\c  /s /mx /xt + 
\c RxDOS.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSCCB.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSDEV.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSEXE.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSFAT.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSFCB.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSFIL.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSLFN.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSF32.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSIFS.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSMEM.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSSFT.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSSTR.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSCTB.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSERR.OBJ + 
\c RxDOSINI.OBJ + 
\c  ,RxDOS.EXE,RxDOS.MAP,;

(The plus sign specifies line continuation to WarpLink.)

The RxDOS.EXE file that is created by the linker (in the obj subdirectory) now needs to
be converted to a flat .COM file. This command line works with OpenWatcom's exe2bin:

\c exe2bin -x -r -l=0 RxDOS.EXE ..\bin\RXDOS.SYS

\S{} Building RxCMD

Similar to RxDOS's build process described in \k{builddosobj}, each of the RxCMD
modules RxCMDCPY, RxCMDDIR, RxCMDFOR, RxCMDPRM, RxCMDREN and RxCMD need to be
assembled into object files first. The
files have to be linked similarly to the RxDOS link process then, too. 
The order of the RxCMD objects probably is important too, the module RxCMD
quite certainly must be specified last. exe2bin is not to be used for RxCMD.

\S{} Building the documentation

The provided documentation is partly written for Halibut, a program that compiles
its input to various other formats. To compile the documentation into one text file
and one HTML file, use this command:

\c halibut --text --html readme.but intro.but changes.but build.but

This writes the readme.txt and readme.htm files.
The order of the input files matters, as the output will reflect this order. Note
that readme.but contains some configuration for the text and HTML output formats
of Halibut, but not for any other formats. You may wish to configure other
output formats if you want to use them.
